    "episode_id": 70792,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/0/70792.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 125,
    "turn_text": "Well, one nice thing about Minnesota is we don't register. You know, we can, all of us, in fact, I would encourage everybody listening to please participate in democracy and don't just vote in the general election, but vote in the primaries. That is, to me, that's the lowest hanging fruit for Americans to actually play a significant role in reforming the status quo. We are letting both parties ultimately elevate candidates who are either too far left, too far right, and the solution to that is available to us. That's voting in primary. So when you do so, you can vote as a Democrat or Republican, choose one ballot or the other. In terms of my affiliation, I'm not going to abandon the party in which I have believed that has the values and principles that I consider to be terribly important. But I think many Democrats, just like me, are recognizing a significant shift in the base of the party that is deeply trying. That is, for going common sense, has shunned pragmatism and is seeing an erosion of competency. And if Democrats become a party of simply protest and opposition without solutions, it's not going to be a party at all. So I am trying my best to be thoughtful and inject some provocations into the conversation. And if that's not successful, I'm going to do what I believe is necessary to ensure that my children and yours and everybody's listening, you know, have a country that is thoughtful, that is competent, that protects the citizens and uses common sense. in its pursuit of problem solving. And right now, I think we'll all agree it is a total unmitigated mess.",
