    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/0/70792.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 129,
    "turn_text": "Well, so here's the good news, Chad, and I think my fellow members of Congress and those in the state legislature in Minnesota would agree, there are a number of outstanding people who participate on our campaigns and service interns and who deeply value public service. When I hear though time and time again, the refrain is I would love to become a city council person, a state legislator, a member of Congress, a senator, except the money that has to be raised to do so, is so obnoxious, and so many outstanding young people just simply don't have the access, the connections, to even consider raising the sums that are necessary, which is why I've made campaign finance reform, you know, a hallmark of my platform. So the good news is we do have a young generation, I think, that is compelled. What we don't have is a system that rewards those people. And frankly, this is where I keep making a case about the duopoly. It's the one thing I think Democrats and Republicans agree on at the national party level is to protect the duopoly. If we can break that down and there can be a compelling alternative party, predicate, uncommon sense, competency, problem solving, and ending the nonsense. And this is the most important element, Chad. a party that would have a significant financial corpus, a big endowment, if you will, that would then allow them that it's to run without raising money. That's what consumes 50% of the time. It makes people trade favors for support. I think it's time for a party that raises money from like-minded Americans, that perhaps is amplified with those of extraordinary means put into an account, and then that party supports candidates to run in races without forcing them to raise hundreds of thousands or even millions of dollars and that allows us to do our jobs rather than enter to the far right, far left and spend all our time raising money which is what too many are doing. I think those two things would make a big difference quickly. And then I think nationally we just have to start elevating schools, families, communities, encouraging young people to consider at least some time in public service. Because we can't take this for granted, Chad, and the erosion of competency is a great concern.",
