    "turn_idx": 138,
    "turn_text": "American should agree that we need to significantly reform our federal government. We need to become more efficient, more effective, ensure that managers are in place of bureaucrats to actually lead agencies. With all that said, If you don't do it in a transparent fashion, and if you don't do it in a way that is legal, how in the world can we see that as a benefit to the United States? And just as everybody listening has been disconcerted by the number of petty thieves and shoplifters that aren't even being charged with crimes, we should hold the federal government to the same standards. If Elon Musk does anything that is illegal, Our judicial system should hold him accountable for the President of the United States. We cannot have a unilateral, we cannot have a unilateral activity of someone outside of the purview of the federal government. But with private data of American citizens doing whatever the heck he wants, I support the notion of reforming the government. I do not support the notion of doing it in dark rooms without transparency and saying the heck with the law. Because we start there, it's a slippery, slippery",
